## Overview
XR controllers and hand tracking raycasters provide spatial interaction mechanisms in immersive environments. The raycaster projects a vector from the controller or hand palm into the virtual scene to perform intersection tests (raycasting) against interactable objects.

Core properties include origin, direction, and maxDistance. Methods involve getRaycaster(), setTransform(), and checkIntersection(). Constraints require frame-by-frame updates within the XRSession animation loop. Raycasters rely on Three.js Raycaster or WebXR hit-test APIs. Hand-tracking integration requires tracking specific joint indices (typically index-tip) for ray emission. Performance is limited by scene complexity and the number of active raycasters. Only one raycaster is recommended per hand/controller to minimize overhead. Base44 environments require efficient management of input sources using the WebXR Controller API to prevent memory leaks in serverless render loops.

